// Jest Snapshot v1, https://jestjs.io/docs/snapshot-testing

exports[`CLI help interface for 'config-manager push oauth-issuers' should be expected english 1`] = `
"Usage: frodo config-manager push oauth-issuers [options] [host] [realm] [username] [password]

[Experimental] Import OAuth2 trusted JWT issuers from the files
realms/<realm>/realm-config/agents/TrustedJwtIssuer/<name>.json that "pull
oauth-issuers" and "pull oauth2-agents" write, from the working directory (-D).
An issuer that does not exist is created and one that does is replaced by the
file. When the server refuses an attribute of a file (it does not know the
attribute), the issuer stays stored without it, nothing is rolled back, the push
names the attributes and fails with exit code 1 unless --allow-failures is
given. Each \${NAME} in a file is filled from -E/--env NAME=value, else from
--env-file, else from the environment variable NAME of the process (a file that
names a variable copies its value into what is pushed). No issuer is ever
deleted. A trusted JWT issuer is a trust anchor: a JWT signed by it is accepted
as a grant, so read the files before you push them.

Arguments:
  host               AM base URL, e.g.: https://cdk.iam.example.com/am. To use a
                     connection profile, just specify a unique substring or
                     alias.
  realm              Realm. Specify realm as '/' for the root realm or 'realm'
                     or '/parent/child' otherwise. (default: "alpha" for
                     Identity Cloud tenants, "/" otherwise.)
  username           Username to login with. Must be an admin user with
                     appropriate rights to manage authentication journeys/trees.
                     If given without a password, and it matches the username
                     already stored in the connection profile for the target
                     host, frodo uses that profile's stored password instead of
                     requiring it on the command line.
  password           Password.

Options:
  --allow-failures   Report operations that fail, but still exit with code 0.
                     Without this option the command runs every operation,
                     prints a summary of what failed and exits with code 1.
  -n, --name <name>  Trusted JWT issuer name (the name of the object, not its
                     iss URL). If specified, pushes only the issuer of the file
                     with that name. Needs a single realm.
  -h, --help         Help
  -hh, --help-more   Help with all options.
  -hhh, --help-all   Help with all options, environment variables, and usage
                     examples.
"
`;
